## Planning and permissions (last verified 2026-09-06)

- New sessions start in plan mode with auto-reviewed approvals. Source writes
  remain disabled; temporary build/cache artifacts use designated writable roots.
- `/execute` or approval of `submit_plan` explicitly enables scoped execution.
  `/plan` revokes child sessions and sandbox processes before enabling planning.
- `/approvals` selects auto review or individual prompts. Hard denial, inherited
  role scope and mode restrictions run before approval; approval cannot widen them.
- Classifier errors/uncertainty prompt the root session; unattended requests deny.
- `ask_user` presents a decision with choices or free text. Approval dialogs are
  once-only; MCP approvals are never cached across a mode change.
- If a process disappears without acknowledging termination, the workflow blocks.
  Check orphan processes and restart; do not treat a lost connection as proof that
  a writer stopped.

`workspace_read`, `workspace_write`, `workspace_edit`, `workspace_bash`,
`workspace_grep`, `workspace_find`, `workspace_ls` and language tools run in SRT
workers. Interactive `!` commands use the same shell boundary. MCP stdio servers
also run inside SRT, while OAuth stays in the trusted host Pi process.

Sensitive paths come from the shared denylist. File tools additionally resolve
symlinks and confine edits to the workspace; git metadata and managed policy/code
are write-protected. This is an explicit denylist, not a guarantee against unknown
credential locations. Add new credential paths to shared policy before exposing
them to a session.

Extensions are trusted host code. Per-tool SRT is not containment against a
malicious extension. No fallback to unsandboxed execution exists in the managed
tools. Linux needs bubblewrap, socat and ripgrep; macOS needs sandbox-exec.

## Specialists, skills and display (last verified 2026-09-06)

Specialists under `~/.pi/agent/agents` render from the shared roster. Each has an
explicit OpenAI model, tool list and mandatory child policy extension. Native
builtin names cannot substitute for missing `workspace_*` tools. Read-only
specialists stay read-only even when the root enters execution.

Use named `subagent` launches with an agent and bounded task. Context is fresh;
MCP-using specialists run in the background. Up to three child sessions share the
root policy. Status, stop, interrupt and non-recovering steer are enabled; arbitrary
workflow scripts, project role overrides, revival and mutating management are not.
Use `bg_wait` in the root for background results. FleetView above the editor shows
ongoing progress without a status command.

Shared skills remain discoverable under `~/.agents/skills`; explicit skill loading
is available through Pi. Tool output starts collapsed: Ctrl+O expands it. MCP
results show a one-line preview. This is keyboard expansion, not per-card mouse
interaction identical to Claude Code.

## MCP and code intelligence (last verified 2026-09-06)

Use the `mcp` proxy to discover and call Context7, Exa, Playwright and Serena.
Configuration is explicit; other harnesses' private configuration is not imported.
MCP scripting, sampling and automatic OAuth discovery are disabled. The approval
broker checks the resolved server/tool/arguments, not just the outer proxy call.

`workspace_lsp_diagnostics` and `workspace_lsp_fix` use configured TypeScript,
Python, Go and Lua servers. The entire plugin call runs inside the sandbox, not
only its language-server subprocess. Project LSP command/root overrides are not
loaded. Serena provides symbol navigation/references and execute-mode rename;
its configuration and project caches are redirected into session scratch.

Claude/Codex proprietary plugins, cloud services and exact UI interactions are
not supplied by these packages. Keep the other harnesses for those capabilities.

## Acceptance checks (last verified 2026-09-06)

From the source repository, run `npm run test:pi`. It covers policy, classifier
fallback, terminal proof, child preflight and secret-stubbed chezmoi projections.
`PI_WORKFLOW_LIVE_TESTS=1 npm run test:pi` additionally exercises actual Unix
sockets and SRT against disposable fixtures; it needs an unrestricted local host.

Before relying on the setup, separately verify subscription login, parent and
foreground/background child model pins, auto approvals, cancellation, FleetView,
MCP queries, diagnostics and approved edits in a disposable project. Fixture
tests do not establish account access or live terminal behavior. Compare Sol and
Astra on matched completed tasks before changing the default model.
